#{[17592186232047 "Recall the following length conversions:\n\n- $1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$\n- $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$\n\nWhen we convert from a smaller unit to a larger unit, we may need to use long division to divide by the number of smaller units in one larger unit.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $84$ feet into yards.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between yards and feet:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nSince we are converting from feet to yards, we divide both sides of the equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $84$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $84{:}$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{84}{3}\n$$\n\nas the division $84 \\div 3$. We can calculate this using long division:\n\n|  | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $6$ |  |\n|  | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n|  |  | $\\fbox{0}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n84\\,\\textrm{ft} = 28\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use long division to simplify the quotients that appear when converting measurements to larger units." #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232048 "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$$360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$$30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards." #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232049 "The **surface area** of a polyhedron is the combined area of all its faces.\n\nTo calculate the surface area of a polyhedron, we use nets.\n\nLet's consider the net of the rectangular pyramid below. What is the surface area of this pyramid?\n\n![](images/1b/1b8db0cb92f9fe3087483e5b0c7077f23a5037ac7f890ecce548b4ce0c4e27ff.png)\n\nThe surface area of our pyramid consists of one rectangle (the base) and four triangles (the side faces).\n\nAlso, notice that there are two different triangular faces:\n\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $60\\,\\text{in}^2$, and\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $30\\,\\text{in}^2$.\n\nCalculating the total area, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot 120in^{2} + 2 \\cdot 60in^{2} + 2 \\cdot 30in^{2} \\\\\n&= 120in^{2} + 120in^{2} + 60in^{2} \\\\\n&= 300in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the surface area of the pyramid is $300 \\, \\text{in}^2$." #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232050 "If we know a polyhedron's measurements, we can calculate its surface area by first drawing the net of the polyhedron. Then, we calculate the areas of its polygonal faces using known formulas and then sum the results.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the surface area of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93d93a9a0dff25bb55a411ba155388f3769487710f2a8d847b0545b113655a02.png)\n\nFirst, we consider the net of our prism.\n\n![](images/aa/aa0cccf3b41ccdfd946f6df6ec3c41eb8ee4c6dbd469b5ca36dd6b5c50d5858b.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the prism is made up of squares and rectangles.\n\n- The rectangles are $8\\,\\text{ft}$ long and $2\\,\\text{ft}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is\n$\\text{Area of rectangle} = 8 \\times 2 = 16 \\, \\text{ft}^2$.\n- The squares have sides of length $2\\, \\text{ft}$. So, the area of one square is\n$\\text{Area of square} = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\, \\text{ft}^2$.\n\nFinally, the surface area of the prism consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 4 \\cdot (Area of rectangle) + 2 \\cdot (Area of square) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 16 + 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 64 + 8 \\\\\n&= 72ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232051 "The **perimeter** of a polygon is the total distance around its boundary. So, to find the perimeter, we add the lengths of all the polygon's sides.\n\nFor example, let's find the perimeter of the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03371bcd082bbda3488418ec0af5b7ffded11b0ce2dd6837c8522c8f1a593c5c.png)\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the triangle is\n\n$$\nP = 12 + 9 + 11 = 32 \\: \\text{yd}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232052 "Now, let's see how to find the perimeter of the rectangle shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8b3b8005a3d2f9294df3048acd0d6fb7186445de56381e5aafc4dacd200cc90.png)\n\nTo find the perimeter, we add the lengths of all the rectangle's sides.\n\nRecall that a rectangle is a quadrilateral with two pairs of equal parallel sides and four right angles.\n\n![](images/77/7734f24982b6147bad1a70d5d7eaec8884fdbc18a2c562a05212cb309b1ff461.png)\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 2 \\cdot (Length + Height) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (9 + 4) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 13 \\\\\n&= 26in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232053 "Recall the following properties of rhombuses and squares:\n\n- A square is a quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides, where *all four sides are equal*, and all four angles are right angles.![](images/9d/9d7f01ef9e4c3bdb2d87c361c6e1aca37be00ffc0be81e446433cf0a9f5e88ff.png)\n- A rhombus is a quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides, where *all four sides are equal*.![](images/55/5584f312d74c98928eb3f368acc89dcbdb2ddf2adab82db75f7b7fb842377a1b.png)\n\nThe special properties of these shapes allow us to find their perimeters even more easily than usual.\n\nFor example, the perimeter of a square with a side of length $7\\:\\text{m}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 4 \\cdot (Side Length) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 28m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232054 "Now, suppose $h=35$. Let's compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}7\n$$\n\nAs before, replacing $h$ with $35$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{h}{7} &=  \\\\\nh \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n35 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 5 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232055 "We can combine several arithmetic operations in linear expressions.\n\nFor example, given that $m = 6$, let's compute $2m + 5$.\n\nReplacing $m$ with $6$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2m + 5 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 6 + 5 &=  \\\\\n12 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 17 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232056 "Finally, let's see how we can evaluate linear expressions in context.\n\nSuppose the temperature (in degrees Celsius) after $t$ hours of a liquid mixture as it cools is given by the expression\n$38 - 4t$.\nWhat is the temperature of the mixture after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nhours?\n\nTo determine the temperature after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nhours, we replace $t$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nand evaluate the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n38 - 4t &=  \\\\\n38 - 4 \\cdot t &=  \\\\\n38 - \\frac{4}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} &=  \\\\\n38 - \\frac{4}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{1} &=  \\\\\n38 - 2 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n38 - 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nhours is $28\\,^\\circ\\textrm{C}$." #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232057 "With the help of a number line, we can place a set of positive and negative whole numbers in order.\nLet's place the following set of numbers from *least* to *greatest*:\n$-3, \\; 1, \\; 2, \\; -1, \\; 4$\n\nFirst, we plot all of these numbers on a number line:\n\n![](images/18/18a86d35ed43e284ca2a9f62e314e253c77128c3ea14cc9e579fdd467da9aefd.png)\n\nRecall that numbers to the left of a value on the line are less than the value.\nSo, going from left to right, we have:\n$-3< -1 < 1 < 2 < 4$\n\nFinally, we can write the numbers in order, from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n-3, \\; -1, \\; 1, \\; 2, \\; 4\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232058 "We can use our knowledge of ordering whole numbers to place rational numbers in order.\nTo do this, we write the numbers with a common denominator, and then compare the numerators.\n\nFor example, let's place the following set of numbers in order, from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}, \\;-3, \\; 1, \\;4\n$$\n\nFirst, we write all of the numbers as fractions with a common denominator of ${\\color{blue}2}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}2}, \\; -\\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}2}, \\; \\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}2}, \\; \\dfrac {8}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNext, we bring any negative signs inside the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}, \\; \\dfrac{(-6)}{2}, \\; \\dfrac{2}{2}, \\; \\dfrac 8 2\n$$\n\nNow, we order the numerators *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-6)}{2} < \\dfrac{2}{2} < \\dfrac 5 2 < \\dfrac{8}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we change each fraction back to its original form:\n\n$$\n-3 < 1 < \\dfrac{5}{2} < 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the numbers in order, from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n-3, \\; 1, \\; \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\; 4\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232059 "A negative number often means a measure in the *opposite direction*. The magnitude of the measure is given by the *absolute value* of the number.\n\nFor instance, suppose a fish is at a position of $-10\\,\\textrm{m}$ relative to sea level.\n\n- The negative sign tells us the direction is *below sea level*, which is the opposite of *above sea level*.\n- To find the actual distance the fish is below sea level (its *depth*), we take the absolute value of its position:\n$\\mid -10 \\mid = 10$\n\nTherefore, the fish is $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ below sea level." #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232060 "We can use vertical number lines, similar to thermometers, to represent measurements that have a *zero point*.\n\nFor instance, *sea level* is a common zero point for measuring altitude and depth.\n\n- Locations *above* sea level have positive values.\n- Locations *below* sea level have negative values.\n\nSuppose a scientist finds a shipwreck $100 \\text{m}$ **below sea level**. We can represent this depth as $-100 \\text{m}$.\n\nThe diagram below shows the location of the shipwreck.\n\n![](images/cc/cc361bd9ec12c09e256105cef4f918832d8ff1cbb707d35e9285befbd3c1f18f.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232061 "Another way to model fractions is with a point on a number line from $0$ to $1$.\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the denominator is the total number of equal parts that the line is split into, and\n- the numerator is the number of steps to the point from $0$ (left to right).\n\nFor example, let's find the fraction represented by the number line below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf2df9409acfb58acd879fb3dfc3f3579a79fd102c6258da246a1cb9226b78e.png)\n\nNotice that the line is split into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts. And the number that we are interested in is located $\\color{red}2$ steps from $0$ (left to right).\n\n![](images/05/05d4093f318810dd2086c2c6166219890a3f557707b89c323ce82cb843854d87.png)\n\nTherefore, the picture represents the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232062 "We can represent mixed numbers with number lines.\n\nFor example, let's write the number shown on the number line below as a mixed number.\n\n![](images/f4/f4e1b05632d359a21c097d114afd51d9005e942c6badcf89871b312d2a3e958f.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/77/77ef5e27b471e072fd3e3be72447325b2b6b49081075f9d52b094438a0345833.png)\n\nWe can land on our point by making $\\color{red}1$ whole step plus $\\color{blue}2$ part steps (out of a possible ${\\color{blue}{3}})$.\n\n![](images/d9/d9d9500d6b8f1fcc000a6c4e06449f789b92c30ed25469862b01e1929e770397.png)\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362403 "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that ![](images/80/80eb0b356da6e6a2315ea1466d34b914c6cad5e59ff07c4d061ea82b69ed9838.svg) and we are asked to find ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo find ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we want to match the expression\n![](images/52/52599a11cea864ea801b87d8ec0ea288252afa4265395d9fbb4a4dc2815e9e7e.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) be the origin, and let ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/a1/a14e933631f2b075c5a0bb4ab14fad2ca2790c3be2122b1fe8f4b7193fb505b2.svg) end at points ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/21/2109f0ae1a3679002930f1bf8bfa3490cf23c540ae6bbd50c093e5064327ab15.svg) as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is ![](images/bd/bdd7762354dbbdecb02ccf8284e0830f5421588bbb7fbf1bd9c3a9268a750649.svg) we first draw the vector ![](images/cb/cbbad9d9d0e183ac112cfdf946b99fd00718e0c6856d1a75564925b0e2760e85.svg) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/a2/a2b2f82b89941e4065ecf4c3bf612ab3d7e66fdb16c848dd2ce169480714e7ae.svg) Then\n\n![](images/3a/3a9a2959c3a256cb2651e0f00b0d7fe774f95aeee92289284bffb4c3b11b843a.svg)\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n![](images/f1/f1e5980924989b13362a77d776ecbd4b7567f99e30fe26eb57ee99d18fa1d464.svg)\n\nAlso, the vector ![](images/5f/5f1f2cecb3a462fc45e60ec717c2fde5b1aaba79448a58aae1a9d3023b21b7bb.svg) has length ![](images/f5/f551620762fb84e33026129595b42ef514082cc2e137b6762d75fd574d56383c.svg) but points in the opposite direction to ![](images/85/853e57b0bf9a8f7c16e59d37575ae23ac4028ed66b30720af9d7d49ab595c91b.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/4f/4fdd6b0f88ff6e5ea11ace72a5b61c24fe8cf6b1d85b4bceee599dde18471b5d.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/3c/3cce8321317b15087f61b778b47963fdff160e7df1e3ade0d33b040e10125994.svg) we get\n\n![](images/71/71d018c5f48d3afb47781ddff61419623e8c6d15856151398905494498254880.svg)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/57897701facaf26cf0826a2b6a12f408ae1acd8a5f4e78575a4457f66a156d9b.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/e6/e6090f180976846ce7a2e6e8d696a0fa078a471c950de6188908528c881839fb.svg) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/79/792ff38b4658d1d4bbfbc57cbe93be95dd8d90b24559f601250eaa81becf8d24.svg) we get\n\n![](images/99/99914dd947bc5c5acbb4d9aa989efb9fef4dc78b0242629ea0508a81e7058f61.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362404 "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/92/92e98c3630891023a50149ee1f15d2bd5d2730e93f166931e86a9e4640ebd1c1.svg) and a parallelogram ![](images/14/14fa5b1a9ad8e22445d2e6ab97a047b0b6dc51912ed129823847560bad78554f.svg) where ![](images/37/37252d39c3cb979bda5aa0e19184009625e32ad6b4d496ec843edcd9ebd11444.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/1e/1ea9147123ec18b9ae6f584584c919a462f7efdee6b5b72b61a1cf3c4b13b88a.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) From the diagram, we can write\n![](images/8c/8c0197f45331c0bfd19524c6044f35ddd1d725512112d9700750d10c8709f89f.svg)\n\nWe know that\n![](images/14/146676b86f179710731d5e1ec1b4e5e5e3cf4cdf1af0d4cd4db3d709a36ea0c6.svg)\n\nAlso, since ![](images/c6/c6dbfaa7b99647ca21ec1ad2c57f563756ed17cec04b559d89a086571f1419a1.svg) is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n![](images/94/940eac51696aa93c499bd105cf5726bbed6eaac94eda297d0743af6cd00a67d3.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/a6/a69eca8aae37a4ecd6d3653c994b2bea69e37a8be6dcb00c4a5eeafbc58f1382.svg) we get\n![](images/f5/f56355c56683deba996e3f8cbdd8a9862cd7a9f6f570be9ab12cd4520748e7ec.svg)\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362405 "Let's look at two examples on the interval ![](images/e2/e22fc73eac428fdb3bfeee00d138cd876e3044ab2a748cc8f40d782aaf434439.svg)\n\n- The function\n![](images/71/71eaeb8069eec5b9ddf0c7eec99da1baeabd61c0b3c9ff649b54508e71893bca.svg)\nis piecewise smooth. We split the interval at ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) into the two open intervals ![](images/15/15b88576287296d0c1943529024b5d5d53fc69cdaead2d5fd52d89c8abb41cb2.svg) and ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg) On these intervals,\n![](images/48/4899d49ad98937c557e2997bafb16377a6895d59136d33861a5aaed9327f3018.svg)\nso\n![](images/d8/d83a825b6cfc3d6e00dbe430592744c9742c1a486c8eff28c9ee128487d78cae.svg)\nBoth ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) and ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) are piecewise continuous on each interval and have finite one-sided limits as ![](images/3b/3b2ddb6a8e51e49c33e713a81c8f2b9385caceac10e365cf29f036bff567cea7.svg) Therefore, ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) is piecewise smooth on ![](images/de/decd98825807a5dd9b294f886554a9903663b70d87c8bc10f83ece44318899ed.svg)\n\n- Now consider the function\n![](images/14/1475e903d9121eb68ed1a3d4e3f5d1aca300c0ee3d630f73734c4ae591f8184e.svg)\nThis function is *not* piecewise smooth on ![](images/e2/e22fc73eac428fdb3bfeee00d138cd876e3044ab2a748cc8f40d782aaf434439.svg) Although ![](images/99/994427fe929fdf538d7c1be792e6fa4b85697f0921161bf5ddf0e639864c9f27.svg) is piecewise continuous, its derivative is not. For ![](images/55/55107a28eeb166a15b291e97dd508b24bef08827157c71cdac355c5caa9b069f.svg) its derivative is\n![](images/e1/e1c27659384614a3800680459e46d3b032469cb4e22340cb9242e53f408784a0.svg)\nThis derivate is continous on the open subintervals ![](images/15/15b88576287296d0c1943529024b5d5d53fc69cdaead2d5fd52d89c8abb41cb2.svg) and ![](images/e2/e20969a0f7b9f86a9eb0054b8bc39d7f69cd11f8a6838a602ca0a22a0aa24d0d.svg) but failed to have finite one-sided limits as ![](images/3b/3b2ddb6a8e51e49c33e713a81c8f2b9385caceac10e365cf29f036bff567cea7.svg)\n\nThis shows an important difference:\n\n- *piecewise continuity* allows finitely many simple discontinuities;\n\n- *piecewise smoothness* requires the function to be smooth on each interval between finitely many breakpoints, and prevents the derivative from becoming unbounded." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362406 "Let's apply the general algorithm to find the power series solution for the following equation:\n\n$$\ny' - y  =0\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we write the solution as a general power series $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Next, we compute $y'$ so that we can substitute the general series solution into the differential equation. Computing $y',$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{'} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{'} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting in the differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-y & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Then, we rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value. In the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:** We consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$ Since both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 5:** Setting the coefficients of the series equal to $0,$ we get the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =0 \\\\ {a}_{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{n+1},\\,n=0,1,2…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the following, we'll see how to apply this recurrence relation to find the final power series solution of the differential equation." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362407 "So far, we have studied *piecewise continuity*, which allows a function to have finitely many simple discontinuities on an interval.\n\nSometimes, however, we need a stronger condition: not only should the function behave well on each piece, but its *derivative* should also behave well on each piece.\n\nA function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) is **piecewise smooth** on ![](images/8e/8efa43e7c8129d56297418824f9aebeef655e3f4c9cb65e7bf4c0a11f9d19208.svg) if both ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) and ![](images/07/07df7613127ce9ff43e0e14018d11cafc18c6307ff61097112b701516707340e.svg) are piecewise continuous on ![](images/1b/1bc9213bac9b06c6c891f5fbc36ca1065df5baf7713810ff84b3e7f8afc36ab6.svg) In other words, ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) is piecewise smooth on ![](images/2f/2f2185d0962aceaafb8dbacf7486e61ccafbb77a54e961b33e886dd3b79a20df.svg) if\n\n- ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) is piecewise continuous on ![](images/88/88e5bb9f44a0b3bb16a7e0e5461c5cc3f1f0bef907eda396d725caf367f29fb5.svg) and\n\n- ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, a piecewise-smooth function may have finitely many breakpoints, but between those points the function and its derivative behave well, and the derivative cannot become unbounded." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362408 "The general power series solution of the differential equation $y' - y  =0$ is\n\n$$\ny =\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n.\n$$\n\nAs we determined previously, the coefficients of this series satisfy the recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =\\dfrac{a_n }{n+1  }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2, \\ldots\n$$\n\nSince we are looking for the general solution of the differential equation, we can set the first term of its power series solution to an arbitrary number. So, we can set $a_0=C,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the recurrence relation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{0+1}=\\frac{C}{1}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1+1}=\\frac{C}{1\\cdot 2}, \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2+1}=\\frac{C}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3}, \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{C}{n!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =C+\\frac{C}{1}\\,x+\\frac{C}{1\\cdot 2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3}\\,{x}^{3}+⋯+\\frac{C}{n!}\\,{x}^{n}+⋯ \\\\  & =C(1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯+\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}+⋯) \\\\  & =C({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall the definition of the standard Maclaurin series of the exponential function:\n\n$$\ne^{x} = 1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}x^2+\\cdots + \\dfrac{x^n}{n!} + \\cdots = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{x^n}{n!}\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we can write the general solution of our differential equation as \n\n$$\ny = C e^x.\n$$\n\nThe solution of a differential equation is not always a recognizable standard series. But if we are able to find the recurrence relation for the coefficients, we can always present the solution as a general power series." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 "When a solid is described in words, we first identify its bounding surfaces and then decide how to describe the entire region with inequalities.\n\nA useful strategy is:\n\n- identify the variable that lies between a lower surface and an upper surface,\n\n- determine the projection of the solid onto a coordinate plane,\n\n- write bounds for the projected region,\n\n- use those bounds to set up a triple integral with integrand equal to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg).\n\nIn many volume problems, the solid lies between a bottom surface\n![](images/8e/8eeea9a9caa745d87342df718b249603d257e35820bea2df1f1d647d61877c0c.svg)\nand a top surface\n![](images/21/211a455fb9c563450b5b5225f0d3615467c89870e467920ad73beacb802375b8.svg)\n\nIn that case, we describe the solid by\n![](images/eb/eb36f1bb66ca8c06c73de972395684c368cc3cdba4d05b9300dee9f852c3174b.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nSo, to find the volume, we use\n![](images/c6/c6fd71393350dac5cd3d1067227863cab1e92534812ea083231408ce660143bf.svg)\n\nSo, the verbal description tells us two things:\n\n- which surface is below and which is above,\n\n- which points ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) belong to the base region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg).\n\nBefore integrating, it is always a good idea to check that the upper surface is actually above the lower surface on the entire projected region." #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 "Consider the volume of the solid region enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, the plane ![](images/af/af57fba1cdb37044c8a4f922ba0ce95fcae5347960e050ac391af840198aca28.svg) and the planes ![](images/89/89c97ad93c125fc2b6f61d44fe9a90845fd289329d79d506c857f970d7586540.svg) ![](images/48/480183a1321e5b4fdff9500a551818f6fb0ab2713c0fdd8fa9df88a640394af9.svg) ![](images/8d/8de04eef03bf4f5fd455bcd39f1d747bc5e38650a1f8c6dd5249166cf5fc022d.svg) and ![](images/ca/ca7dadeea1df352335554e1c48b6398167035636994354744737e4c75a1b0134.svg)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region.\n\nFirst, the phrase **enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane** tells us that the bottom of the solid is\n![](images/ef/efcfb7a56c85727ded8a9d5c9bd75fd79ac0bc20ba202c2da6aaca3c4e3ba503.svg)\n\nThe plane\n![](images/21/211cfe0529803c8450173dbcaa60c66f276389b5944047bc390738703f657309.svg)\nis the top of the solid.\n\nAlso, the planes\n![](images/a3/a3fe77f111727e68d20276851f67f7fc662eaea5df49eaf96695a9234f6304bf.svg)\ndescribe the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. So the base region is the rectangle\n![](images/59/5925cb0d118a2a3457f4683c7b38bfa59bc5e8bbc106af32f0364a0207f9af25.svg)\n\nTherefore, for each point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in this rectangle, the variable ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) runs from the bottom surface to the top surface:\n![](images/b1/b1af88023edd0b854bb6b598521693ebf4e941d8a5567e6801801f1deeacb902.svg)\n\nSo the volume is\n![](images/bd/bd9acbbd0fb9472aafdc28fe38d7cf6a103c123ff999116cefca75f8dbf624a8.svg)\n\nNow we evaluate:\n\n![](images/fa/fa67106261e7d847a0be115b74ca3ebaa5d5aff7ceca30b0b94b950fb1b741cd.svg)\n\nTherefore, the volume of the solid is ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)\n\nIn problems like this, the planes involving only ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) usually describe the base region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, while the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane and the surface or plane involving ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) describe the lower and upper bounds for ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)." #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362411 "Alternatively, we can conceptualize the volume of a solid using a **triple integral**. \n\nRevisiting the previous example enclosed between the paraboloids, our solid can be formally written as a 3D region:\n![](images/17/17cee1ab33ee8dcd73e0c3a0151c563a6c64ca1c898383dbbca4d8ac7f1e0ff5.svg)\n\nThis means that for each point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in the projection region ![](images/87/87e7e0743ecd6609730f4b9a9b3681b1f360ca86bcb209e03561a197b18782f3.svg) the variable ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) runs from the lower surface \n![](images/6a/6a37b724b57c8f5650e5970d1f90da49fe6a482582c7776aa567d41c2f6c406b.svg)\nup to the upper surface \n![](images/58/585d8d094c7eea9f1d7b242bebd015c27b39692e2cddbbaa76c0a87ce65b3ea5.svg)\n\nThe volume of ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) is given by evaluating a triple integral of ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) over the region. By computing the innermost integral with respect to ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg), this calculation logically reduces to the double integral formula:\n![](images/8e/8e024d3e6c21346148c18139918dc5780683039bb34749489e188740ec3fa836.svg)\n\nIf we write ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) using ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) as the outer variable, then\n![](images/d4/d4cdea1370f4184ff8a5387dd95ed1cedd1b81d6027c32eea9240549d0dff0a4.svg)\nso\n![](images/10/109e5d163609178b9c69ae87bd236d3e0a8830157556082f92f2933c8b6d215b.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362412 "Sometimes a solid is enclosed between two surfaces. In these cases, we can compute its volume using a double integral over the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nSuppose a solid is enclosed between the paraboloids\n![](images/7b/7b63c2c4326ca1b7c5001ce0fbed7a7f0e4c5f54a8b4b26eca446f2b2b65dfa5.svg)\n\nTo compute its volume, we use\n![](images/83/83fed741dc600a6aa368543b2522f90191579e318c653b03a6924fc9ffa1868d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nFirst, we find where the two surfaces intersect. This gives the boundary of the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane:\n![](images/3d/3d710d141ba98ae84f8c82b8ba0233781fa3627b639857710981c6c657fac203.svg)\n\nSo, the projection region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the disk\n![](images/d5/d5eef3331fa2e621c6a523f562fc759540299b92a24dfb1502b0d73c11377157.svg)\n\nThe height of the solid is the difference between the upper surface (![](images/cd/cdac8cf874dd0689e35a1c8d1a790bd5279d33ca80f0fd6fb4a2034e4a8b35f4.svg)) and the lower surface (![](images/69/6963e5988546516a47de0a33528be8c2c1b4626d97d9730e8bb950aa9100fe92.svg)):\n![](images/50/50c6958184049a24a56265775b5500b62bac9face27b2893824ed7d1b58d70ec.svg)\n\nIf we write ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) using ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) as the outer variable, then\n![](images/67/671080e239a3f478bcd6b47f0b6b31bdd3bef18de65edc92ac956d4a5cf07683.svg)\n\nThe volume is given by the iterated integral:\n![](images/18/180f43f3029d64ca0fe7971d108167a900d05ddbaa9aed0e017e3ef85cf2c279.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 "If we wish to find the flux through a tilted plane, we should first calculate the unit normal vector to this plane.\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle x,\\: 1-x+y,\\: 2y+8\\rangle$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=2x+2y-1$ such that $\\textrm{Area}(S) = 2.$ \nAssume that we need to find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\nIf $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = -1, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2(1-x+y)+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2+2x-2y+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362414 "Quiz 3\n\n- XP is awarded based on performance, with the addition of bonus XP for a perfect score.\n\n- An XP penalty will be assigned for poor performance, and the worse the score, the larger the penalty.\n\n- Once the quiz has been started, the timer will continue to run." #edb/ref 13194139533446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362415 "Quiz 24\n\n- XP is awarded based on performance, with the addition of bonus XP for a perfect score.\n\n- An XP penalty will be assigned for poor performance, and the worse the score, the larger the penalty.\n\n- Once the quiz has been started, the timer will continue to run." #edb/ref 13194139533446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362416 "Differential Equations: Placement Exam\n\n- Response time is factored into the scoring, but don't rush! What's most important \n            is that you answer correctly any questions that you understand.\n\n- You can take a break in between questions. Each question is timed independently.\n\n- Exiting a question before answering it will result in the question \n            being marked as incorrect.\n\n- If you don't know how to answer a question, click the **Don't Know** button. \n            Guessing will only make the results less accurate.\n\n- Expect to get wrong answers. This is not a performance assessment, but an adaptive \n            diagnostic that is attempting to find the contour of your knowledge profile." #edb/ref 13194139533446 #edb/ref 1759218