ns, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." #edb/ref 13194139533506 #edb/ref 17592186045419] [17592186405607 "**Math 255 — Recitation Worksheet 2**\n\nDue: 2026-10-06\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-2%2F255-R2.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGraded for completeness. Work on the printed worksheet, write/type on the PDF, or use your own paper with the same layout. Provide first name, last name, and OSU student ID on the cover. Submit one PDF to Gradescope under **Recitation Worksheet 2**, preserving the six-page template: cover followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. Scan printed work; digital work already has the required format. For separate paper, photograph all pages and merge them; combine multiple pages for one problem into a single page (a collage). Incorrectly formatted submissions receive 0. No due date is stated in the PDF." #edb/ref 13194139533506 #edb/ref 17592186045419] [17592186405632 "**6.2 Line Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-10-15\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/cad9d5c1-5041-449d-b720-8f2e67d796cc)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers with symbolic notation and fractions as needed. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. All 24 assigned questions are retained; questions 7 and 8 each have separate dx and dy answer slots. The live platform allows unlimited attempts without an incorrect-attempt penalty and submissions up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." #edb/ref 13194139533506 #edb/ref 17592186045419] [17592186405772 "**6.3 Conservative Vector Fields**\n\nDue: 2026-10-22\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/3a627574-021f-4d0f-9969-54703a59bf12)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers, using symbolic notation and fractions where needed. Preserve K or C as requested for constants. Questions 1–4 explicitly require the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. The platform allows unlimited attempts with no incorrect-attempt penalty; submissions up to 50 days late incur a 2% daily penalty." #edb/ref 13194139533506 #edb/ref 17592186045419] [17592186405909 "Date imported: 2026-09-30\nDue: 2026-10-02\n\nSource: [WeBWorK — WebWork 1](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/)\n\nSix problems, each worth 1 point. The equations and initial conditions below are transcribed from the assigned problem versions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." #edb/ref 13194139533506 #edb/ref 17592186045419] [17592186405957 "**5.7 Change of Variables in Multiple Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-11-12\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d5d2efff-1127-4196-836e-e717fd7546a3)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers using symbolic notation and fractions. Use the stated substitutions in Questions 7–9 and 11; use a change of variables in Question 12. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late with a 2% daily penalty." #edb/ref 13194139533506 #edb/ref 17592186045419] [17592186405987 "Date: 2026-09-29\nDue: 2026-09-29\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR1%2F255-R1.pdf)\n\n## Instructions\n\nRecitation worksheets are graded for completeness. Complete the worksheet on the printed pages, digitally on the PDF, or on your own paper with the same problem layout. Submit a single PDF to Gradescope under **Recitation Worksheet 1**, with the cover page followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. If a solution spans multiple pages, merge those pages into a single page for that problem. See the original PDF for the full submission instructions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." #edb/ref 13194139533506 #edb/ref 17592186045419] [17592186484428 "**MTH 255 — Recitation Worksheet 3**\n\nDue: 2026-10-15\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline verified: 2026-10-12\nDeadline source: [Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064132/assignments/10705971)\nDate imported: 2026-10-12\nSource: [255-R3.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-3%2F255-R3.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and worked explanations are authored additions; source-provided answers are identified where used.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThe worksheet is graded for completeness. Show calculations and give the requested graphical reflections. Complete the PDF, annotate it digitally, or use separate paper laid out like the template. Upload one six-page PDF to Gradescope under Recitation Worksheet 3, including the cover and one page per problem. Include blank problems and combine long solutions onto their allotted page." #edb/ref 13194139535240 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186484453 "**MTH 341 — Written Homework 2**\n\nDue: 2026-10-14\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline verified: 2026-10-12\nDeadline source: [Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/assignments/10695094)\nDate imported: 2026-10-12\nSource: [341-WHW2.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FWHW-2%2F341-WHW2.pdf)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra, Version 2017 Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler. Exercise wording is from this CC BY textbook.\n\nThe downloaded assignment sheet bears an old Spring 2022/2023 heading and an April 13 deadline; these are historical source details. The current deadline above is verified in Fall 2026 Canvas.\n\nStudy answers and worked explanations are authored additions; source-provided answers are identified where used.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse separate 8.5×11-inch or A4 paper, and use the assignment sheet as the cover with your name and student ID. Submit on Canvas; the sheet states that late work is not accepted. Do calculations and row operations by hand. Problem 16 must use a different matrix from the textbook answer. Show required explanations and proofs." #edb/ref 13194139535240 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186484490 "**MTH 256 — Written Homework 2**\n\nDue: 2026-10-16\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline verified: 2026-10-12\nDeadline source: [Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064169/assignments/10722761)\nDate imported: 2026-10-12\nSource: [256-WHW2.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FWHW-2%2F256-WHW2.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and worked explanations are authored additions; source-provided answers are identified where used.\n\n## Instructions\n\nShow your work and retain the source exercise numbers. The authored study explanations use the standard local criteria: continuity of $f$ on a neighborhood guarantees existence, and continuity of both $f$ and $f_y$ on a neighborhood guarantees uniqueness. The partial-derivative prerequisite is a verified MVC lesson outside the usual MTH 256 route. In Problems 1–2, determine where the cited theorem guarantees existence or uniqueness; do not equate failure of its hypotheses with failure of a solution. For Problems 3–4 use the supplied homogeneous-equation substitution." #edb/ref 13194139535240 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186484525 "**MTH 256 — Recitation Activity 3**\n\nDue: 2026-10-14\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline verified: 2026-10-12\nDeadline source: [Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064169/assignments/10722757)\nDate imported: 2026-10-12\nSource: [256-R3.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-3%2F256-R3.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and worked explanations are authored additions; source-provided answers are identified where used.\n\n## Instructions\n\nProblems 1–3 use separable equations. Their chemistry background is supplied for context and is not assessed. Solve the stated exercises, using the given substitutions in Problems 4–5." #edb/ref 13194139535240 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186484616 "**MTH 255 — Written Homework 3**\n\nDue: 2026-10-15\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline verified: 2026-10-12\nDeadline source: [Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064132/assignments/10705972)\nDate imported: 2026-10-12\nSource: [255-WHW3.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-3%2F255-WHW3.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and worked explanations are authored additions; source-provided answers are identified where used.\n\n## Instructions\n\nShow clear calculations or written explanations; answers alone do not earn full credit. Complete the printed PDF, annotate it digitally, or use separate paper laid out like the template. Submit one six-page PDF to Gradescope under Written Homework 3: the cover page and one page for each problem, including blank problems. If a solution spans several pages, combine them onto its single submission page." #edb/ref 13194139535240 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186486524 "On a number line, a number and its *additive inverse* are at the same distance from zero but on opposite sides. Remember that the distance of a number from zero is given by its *absolute value*.\n\nFor instance, the only two numbers that are at a distance of $4$ from zero are $4$ and $-4.$ So, $-4$ is the additive inverse of $4$ (and vice versa).\n\n![](images/c0/c0ac86b7f6b42079f164e07e9c0f7c8e35589c1eb8b1fe8762094ff1a59d0d5c.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486525 "Now, let $p=6$ and $q=-9.$ What point on the number line corresponds to $p+q?$\n\nThe value of $p+q$ is the number located a distance $|q|$ from $p$ on the number line, \n\n- moving to the *right* if $q$ is *positive*, and\n\n- moving to the *left* if $q$ is *negative*.\n\nIn our situation:\n\n- We start at $p = 6$ on the number line.\n\n- Here, $q = -9$, so the distance we move is $|q| = 9.$\n\n- Because $q$ is *negative*, we move $9$ units to the *left* from $6.$\n\n![](images/26/265469a65efa2ae63d794ee2ad0eeb79c6754ab18a7bec3791b8ba05007a744e.png)\n\nThis point corresponds to the number\n\n$$\n6 + (-9) = -3.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486526 "Suppose that a bank account had an *initial balance* of $\\[math]0.$ During the day, there was a *withdrawal* of $\\[/math]80.$ Then there was a *deposit* of $\\[math]80$ later.  \n\nWhat is the resulting amount in the account?\n\nIn our situation:\n\n- During the day, the account had a *withdrawal of* $\\[/math]80,$ which is a change of $-\\[math]80.$\n\n- Later, the account had a *deposit of* $\\[/math]80,$ which is a change of $+\\[math]80.$\n\nThese two changes are *opposite quantities*. When opposite quantities combine, they make $0{:}$\n\n$$\n-80 + 80 = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the balance returns to its original value of $\\[/math]0.$" #edb/ref 13194139535252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489385 "A rational number can have a decimal representation that either *ends* or *repeats*.\n\nIn this lesson, we will see how to convert rational numbers written as fractions into decimals using *long division*. \nWe begin with examples where the decimal terminates. \n\nLet's convert $\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into a decimal using long division.\n\nTo write the fraction as a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n$$\n7 \\div 2\n$$\n\nWe proceed using long division as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,3    {\\, . \\,} 5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7   {\\, . \\,} 0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{2} = 3.5.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489386 "The key takeaway for this lesson is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{3} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0.\\overset{―}{78} & =\\frac{26}{33} \\\\ 0.333\\overset{―}{67} & =\\frac{16517}{49500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." #edb/ref 13194139535272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489387 "It's convenient to use the following special **bar-notation** to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely:\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{78} & =0.78\\,78\\,78\\,78… \\\\ 0.\\overset{―}{675} & =0.675\\,675\\,675…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.45\\overline{67} = 0.45\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489388 "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTo find its decimal representation, we divide $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"4\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\:\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"4\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\, . \\,} 0  {\\:\\phantom{|}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal** - a decimal in which the digits after the decimal point continue forever in a *repeating pattern*." #edb/ref 13194139535272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493025 "**MTH 341 — Week 2 Graded Group Discussion**\n\nDue: 2026-10-09\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/groups/750466/discussion_topics/11698215)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThis is a graded discussion in the Class group. Create and post both original matrix examples and your explanations, then comment on at least one classmate’s work. Rubric: 3 points for creating and posting both matrices, 1 point for correct examples or only minor errors, and 1 point for an appropriate peer response. Missing work earns no points for that component; major errors earn 0 for correctness, and an inappropriate peer response earns 0 for the peer component." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493057 "**MTH 341 — Post-reading Questions: §§2.1.7–2.1.8**\n\nDue: 2026-10-13\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/quizzes/3193299)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\nReferenced reading: [Theorems 2.41 and 2.42, printed page 78](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=90).\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions. Personal self-assessment choices are unkeyed; answer them from your own experience.\n\n## Instructions\n\nRead the assigned textbook sections, then answer the questions to the best of your ability. Self-reports about familiarity and confidence have no objectively correct choice." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493070 "Source question: Review item 1, PDF page 1.\n\nReview **all lecture materials and written homeworks**; the source says these form a large portion of the review. This numbered item is a study direction, not a separate mathematical question. The supplied solutions point to in-class activity and written-homework solutions in the Canvas modules." #edb/ref 13194139535303 #edb/ref 17592186045418] [17592186493118 "**MTH 341 — Post-reading Questions: §§1.2.2–1.2.3**\n\nDue: 2026-10-02\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/quizzes/3193294)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\nReferenced reading: [Algorithm 1.19, printed page 19](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=31).\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions. Personal self-assessment choices are unkeyed; answer them from your own experience.\n\n## Instructions\n\nRead the assigned textbook sections, then answer the questions to the best of your ability. Self-reports about familiarity and confidence have no objectively correct choice." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493133 "**MTH 341 — Post-reading Questions: §§1.2.3–1.2.4**\n\nDue: 2026-10-06\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/quizzes/3193312)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nRead the assigned textbook sections, then answer the questions to the best of your ability." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493146 "**MTH 341 — Week 1 Graded Group Discussion**\n\nDue: 2026-10-02\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/groups/750466/discussion_topics/11698203)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThis is a graded discussion in the Class group. Create and post your own original example and explanation, then respond to at least one classmate. Rubric: 3 points for creating and posting the required system, 1 point for a correct example or only minor errors, and 1 point for an appropriate peer response. Missing work earns no points for that component; major errors earn 0 for correctness, and an inappropriate peer response earns 0 for the peer component." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493148 "**MTH 341 — Mini Exam 1 Review**\n\nDue: not applicable (exam review)\nTest date: 2026-10-16\nFilename date: test deadline\nExam window: 2026-10-15 through 2026-10-16\nExam deadline: 2026-10-16, 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Mini Exam 1 schedule](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/assignments/10695694)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\nQuestion PDF: [MTH341-Mini-Exam1_Review.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FSG-1%2FMTH341-Mini-Exam1_Review.pdf)\nSupplied solutions: [ME1 review solutions.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FSG-1%2FME1%20review%20solutions.pdf)\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nMini Exam 1 covers textbook §§1.1.1–2.1.8. The review is not exhaustive: revisit the lecture materials and written homeworks and work additional similar problems when needed. The guide itself has no submission deadline; its filename uses the verified October 16 exam deadline. Show examples, reasoning, and proofs where requested. Authored solutions were checked independently and compared with the supplied handwritten key." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493150 "**MTH 341 — Post-reading Questions: §§2.1.9–2.1.10**\n\nDue: 2026-10-14\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/quizzes/3193298)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions. Personal self-assessment choices are unkeyed; answer them from your own experience.\n\n## Instructions\n\nRead the assigned textbook sections, then answer the questions to the best of your ability. Self-reports about familiarity and confidence have no objectively correct choice." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493166 "**MTH 341 — Post-reading Questions: §§2.1.1–2.1.3**\n\nDue: 2026-10-07\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/quizzes/3193313)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nRead the assigned textbook sections, then answer the questions to the best of your ability." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186493170 "**MTH 341 — Post-reading Questions: §§2.1.3–2.1.7**\n\nDue: 2026-10-09\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-13\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/quizzes/3193311)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and worked examples are authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nRead the assigned textbook sections, then answer the questions to the best of your ability." #edb/ref 13194139535304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186495277 "Now, suppose that installing a fence costs $\\[math]360$ per yard. What would be the unit price per foot?\n\nFirst, notice that there are $3$ feet in $1$ yard.  \n\nTo find the unit price per foot, we *divide* the price per yard by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n360 \\div 3 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, installing the fence costs $\\[/math]120$ per foot.\n\nIn reverse, if we know that installing a fence costs $\\[math]120$ per foot, to find the unit price per yard, we would need to *multiply* the price per foot by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n120 \\times 3 = 360\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
